diff --git a/system/gd/rust/linux/HFP_PCM_BRIDGE.md b/system/gd/rust/linux/HFP_PCM_BRIDGE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..55f7a9f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/system/gd/rust/linux/HFP_PCM_BRIDGE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,193 @@
+# Linux HFP PCM bridge
+
+This integration exposes the existing Floss software SCO audio path to a desktop audio
+server. Floss already decodes microphone SCO packets to PCM and encodes playback
+PCM to SCO in `stack/btm/btm_sco.cc` and `btm_sco_hci.cc`. mSBC and LC3 processing
+remain in Floss. For CVSD, the controller performs the codec conversion and the
+host HCI path carries PCM. No Bluetooth codec is required in PipeWire.
+
+The existing Unix stream socket is full duplex:
+
+```
+/var/run/bluetooth/audio/.sco_data
+```
+
+Write speaker PCM to this socket; read microphone PCM from it. PCM is signed
+16-bit little-endian, mono, at 8000 Hz for CVSD, 16000 Hz for mSBC, or 32000 Hz
+for LC3. The initial desktop bridge supports CVSD and mSBC only. The existing
+Floss LC3 software path is reported accurately when used by another caller but
+is not enabled by the standard-HCI transport opt-in below.
+
+## Transport prerequisites
+
+The normal Linux integration retains its existing management operations
+`0x0100` (codec capabilities) and `0x0101` (SCO connection/codec notification).
+These operations are not assumed available on a stock distribution kernel.
+
+For a controller transport independently verified to carry SCO packets while
+Floss owns the controller through `HCI_CHANNEL_USER`, the **btadapterd service
+process** can explicitly opt into software HCI transport by setting:
+
+```
+FLOSS_HFP_SOFTWARE_HCI_TRANSPORT=true
+```
+
+It is read when the HFP HAL initializes, so restart the adapter daemon after
+changing it. The environment variable, when present, overrides the process-local
+`bluetooth.hfp.software_hci_transport.enabled` property. Only the exact string
+`true` enables it; the default is disabled. This option:
+
+- Avoids both custom management commands.
+- Disables SCO offload and enables CVSD when the controller advertises SCO.
+- Advertises transparent mSBC only when the controller advertises EV3 packets,
+  using Floss's existing 60-byte software packet framing.
+- Does not advertise LC3/SWB in this mode.
+
+Controller feature bits do **not** prove that a Linux transport works. In
+particular, a USB driver may require the management notification to select its
+isochronous interface while the controller is in user-channel mode. Bypassing
+the notification does not implement that driver work. UART and USB transports
+must be validated individually; this change does not establish ordinary USB
+adapter compatibility. Do not enable this option solely because a headset
+supports mSBC. No kernel/hardware validation has been performed.
+
+## D-Bus contract
+
+Use service `org.chromium.bluetooth`, object
+`/org/chromium/bluetooth/hciN/media`, interface
+`org.chromium.bluetooth.BluetoothMedia`.
+
+`GetHfpPcmConfig(address: s) -> a{sv}` returns:
+
+| Key | D-Bus type | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `ready` | `b` | This address has a connected, software SCO PCM listener with a known format. |
+| `active` | `b` | Any owned audio reservation, SCO connection, connection/disconnection operation, or pending listener is active, regardless of the requested address. |
+| `sample_rate` | `u` | Actual live PCM rate; meaningful when ready. |
+| `bits_per_sample` | `y` | 16 when ready. |
+| `channels_count` | `y` | 1 when ready. |
+| `codec` | `u` | HFP bit ID: CVSD=1, mSBC=2, LC3=4. |
+| `socket_path` | `s` | The socket above when ready; otherwise empty for an unavailable native snapshot. |
+| `generation` | `t` | Process-local transport generation; changes on opening, cleanup or unexpected PCM closure/failure. |
+
+The native snapshot uses the **selected SCO connection's actual codec** and
+address, under a mutex. It is not inferred from advertised remote capabilities.
+Offloaded connections and different addresses do not produce a ready PCM
+snapshot. Readiness is additionally gated by the media service's connected
+audio state. A ready snapshot is an observation, not a lease.
+
+The media interface supplies two-phase, caller-owned audio sessions for both
+A2DP and HFP. The `owner` argument below is a BluetoothMediaCallback object path;
+the D-Bus projection captures its authenticated unique sender, not a bus name
+provided in the message body. Tokens never repeat within the daemon lifetime.
+
+| Method | Input signature | Reply | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `ReserveAudioSession` | `sbo` (address, HFP flag, owner) | `t` | Reserve without starting audio; zero refuses a busy/unready device. |
+| `StartAudioSession` | `toh` (token, owner, notification FD) | `b` | Start the reserved address; HFP selects software CVSD/mSBC. |
+| `StopAudioSession` | `to` (token, owner) | `b` | Cancel even before readiness; stale tokens are harmless no-ops. |
+| `GetAudioSessionPosition` | `to` (token, owner) | `a{sv}` | Owned A2DP byte/timestamp feedback; invalid for HFP or a replaced/inactive session. |
+
+The position dictionary contains `valid: b`, `total_bytes_read: t`,
+`data_position_sec: x`, `data_position_nsec: x`, and `remote_delay_report_ns: t`.
+The timestamp uses CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW. Byte count and timestamp are one locked
+snapshot; an admitted new stream resets them to zero. A rejected/duplicate
+start does not reset another stream. Native active-device identity and Started
+state are checked on the stack main thread before returning a valid snapshot.
+The A2DP feeding format still requires independent verification; these counters
+do not negotiate it.
+
+Recommended client sequence:
+
+1. Register media callbacks and authenticate the unique Floss owner. Require
+   the chosen headset's HFP service-level connection (or A2DP profile) to exist.
+2. Reserve and retain the returned nonzero token **before** starting transport.
+   An unstarted reservation expires after ten seconds. Losing the owner bus
+   connection cancels its reservation/session automatically.
+3. Start with the token, callback path and notification FD. Acceptance is not
+   completion: for HFP a nonzero byte is a codec wakeup, not authoritative PCM
+   configuration; for A2DP byte one reports Started. Zero means failure.
+4. For HFP, retry the getter and socket connection after notification within a
+   bounded deadline. Connected notification can precede listener creation.
+5. Confirm the same ready address-specific format and generation around socket
+   connection before publishing nodes or transferring PCM.
+6. Drain microphone PCM continuously and supply playback PCM. Incoming SCO
+   clocks native transmit. On failure, stop both graph nodes.
+7. Always cancel using the retained token on shutdown, timeout, or cancellation,
+   including before a ready generation is observed. Close the PCM socket and
+   bus connection. Never direct cleanup calls at a replacement daemon owner.
+
+Legacy active-device, codec-configuration, start/stop and media-cleanup calls
+cannot mutate a reserved session. Internal qualification/telephony start and
+stop paths obey the same exclusion; their original behavior remains available
+without an owned session. A stopping session retains exclusion until terminal
+native state, and late Connected/Started callbacks retry cancellation. A2DP
+cancellation before Started disconnects that address's profile because legacy
+native failed starts do not always issue a terminal AudioState callback.
+This may require reconnecting the headset after a cancelled A2DP start. Floss
+also emits terminal HFP failure when policy rejects an accepted open, or when
+codec negotiation is cancelled/fails before a controller connection exists.
+The negotiation timer is cancelled and stale replies outside the matching
+negotiation state are ignored.
+
+Native A2DP selection rejects a streaming source before changing the peer, and
+start/stop recheck the selected address on the stack main thread. Native task
+waits are bounded. If a queued start or stop has an indeterminate result, it is
+treated as accepted so cancellation retains ownership rather than freeing a
+potentially live request. Runtime validation of these races remains required.
+
+## Socket behavior and permissions
+
+The socket retains Floss's `0770` mode and `bluetooth-audio` group. Deployment
+must create the runtime directory and grant only the intended audio process
+access. D-Bus policy must permit Floss's callback method calls to that process.
+The bridge's cooperative lock, active-session ownership and service sandbox
+are deployment responsibilities; this getter grants no additional permissions.
+
+Failure to create the PCM listener immediately requests teardown of the exact
+connected SCO link, without waiting for receive traffic. Readiness publication
+checks the live listener while excluding the UIPC close callback. Microphone
+writes remain nonblocking and suppress SIGPIPE; short writes, write errors and
+unexpected read-side EOF/closure invalidate readiness and advance generation.
+They schedule exact-address/generation teardown on the owning stack thread.
+Expected cleanup invalidates state before joining UIPC, so its close callback
+cannot deadlock cleanup or tear down a newer transport incarnation.
+
+The existing bounded playback read and codec/packet-loss handling remain in
+Floss. This first integration deliberately terminates on PCM write backpressure
+instead of implementing another audio buffering/clock controller. Actual
+latency, loss recovery, kernel transport behavior and headset interoperability
+remain unverified on physical hardware. Build and isolated mock-test evidence
+does not establish physical controller, radio or headset behavior. These checks
+remain separate from hardware validation.
+
+## Automatic desktop discovery and A2DP format
+
+`GetConnectedAudioDevices() -> aa{sv}` returns the same `BluetoothAudioDevice`
+records supplied by `OnBluetoothAudioDeviceAdded`, restricted to bonded, fully
+connected media devices. Register callbacks before taking this snapshot so a
+client can reconcile both initial state and subsequent add/remove events.
+
+`GetA2dpPcmConfig(token: t, owner: o) -> a{sv}` requires the caller-owned live
+A2DP session established by `ReserveAudioSession` / `StartAudioSession`. Its
+fields are `ready: b`, `sample_rate: u`, `bits_per_sample: y`,
+`channels_count: y`, `generation: t`, and `socket_path: s`. The native snapshot
+is populated from the successfully initialized SBC/AAC encoder's actual PCM
+feeding parameters; codec capabilities and requested preferences are not a PCM
+format. `ready` is false for a wrong token/owner, stopped session, wrong native
+active peer, or an uninitialized encoder.
+
+Wait for a ready snapshot before opening the PCM endpoint. Monitor it during
+playback and rebuild the stream on a changed generation or format; generation
+increments on initialization, reconfiguration, and cleanup. It is scoped to a
+daemon instance and must not survive a D-Bus owner change. PipeWire resamples
+its graph audio into this format, so the user does not choose a feeder rate.
+A remote START that precedes reservation can be adopted only for the exact
+same peer when no competing lease exists.
+
+The Linux AAC source uses Floss's upstream MMC/FFmpeg encoder service. Its
+currently advertised AAC source format is 44.1 kHz, stereo, signed 16-bit PCM;
+48 kHz AAC source advertisement remains disabled by upstream. SBC remains the
+mandatory fallback. The codec service uses a private runtime directory,
+bounded worker slots, bounded abandoned reservations, and validates AAC frame
+sizes before transcoding. Desktop clients never call the codec service.
